course: dictionary
lesson: 1398
title: A word from the Silk Road
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words only appear in stories about long ago or faraway places. Caravanserai is one of them. You may not know it, but the words around it give clues. «Caravans of two-humped Bactrian camels crossed cold deserts from one oasis to the next, resting at inns called caravanserais.» Camels, deserts and resting tell you a caravanserai is a place where travellers stop.
explain: Look at the clue «inns». An inn is a place where travellers stay for the night, so a caravanserai is a kind of inn. The sentence also says they were about a day's march apart, so travellers stopped there after a whole day of walking.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: «...resting at inns with big courtyards, where travellers stop for the night.» It still makes sense, so your meaning is right. The word can also mean a group of people travelling together, but that meaning is rare and mostly found in old books, so use the place meaning first.
fact: A caravanserai is an inn with a big courtyard on old trade routes in Asia, the Middle East and North Africa.
fact: Travellers and their camels or horses could rest there for the night.
fact: Merchants on the Silk Road stopped at caravanserais to sleep, trade, water their animals and stay safe from robbers.
fact: «Caravanserais» is the plural of caravanserai.
fact: A rare, literary meaning of caravanserai is a group of people travelling together.
exercise: «Caravans of camels crossed the desert and rested at inns called caravanserais.» What is a caravanserai? | A camel's special saddle | *A kind of inn where travellers rest | A type of desert plant | A tall tower for watching the sky
exercise: Where would merchants on the Silk Road stop to sleep and water their animals? | In a school hall | *At a caravanserai | At a lighthouse | At the top of a mountain
exercise: «Caravanserais, inns spaced about a day's journey apart, gave caravans safe places to rest.» Find and copy one word that tells you caravanserais were inns. | journey | safe | caravans | *inns
test: Which place would a caravan of merchants most likely stop at to rest for the night on an old desert trade route? | *A caravanserai | A railway station | A swimming pool | A ski lodge
test: What is the rare, literary meaning of caravanserai? | A deep well in the sand | A tax paid to a king | *A group of people travelling together | A kind of camel saddle
exam: What does the clue «a day's journey apart» suggest about caravanserais? | *They were spaced a day's travel apart, so travellers could rest each night | They were only for camels, not people | They were built underwater | A swimming pool stood in every town
